<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bad Buchau</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bad Buchau is a small spa town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-swabia">Upper Swabia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federsee">Federsee</a>. It grew beside an abbey of noble canonesses founded in the 8th century and was a free imperial city until 1803. The surrounding Federsee moor is known for its prehistoric pile-dwelling settlements, part of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bad-buchau.jpg" alt="Bad Buchau" width="320" height="182" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photo: Andreas Praefcke · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bad_Buchau_Kappel_Blick_von_Oggelshausen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bad Buchau lies in the district of Biberach near Lake Federsee, from which a wide reed belt separates it. From the 13th century until the mediatisation of 1803 Buchau was the seat of both an Imperial Abbey and a Free Imperial City, each independent of the other. In area it was one of the smallest self-ruling cities of its kind, and its island situation meant it needed no walls or towers. It lost that protection when the level of the lake was lowered, in 1787 and again in 1808; the hope of gaining farmland by this was disappointed, though roads could now be built.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Buchau Abbey was founded in 770 by the Frankish Count Warin and his wife Adelindis, and received grants from the Frankish kings <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a> in 819 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a> in 857. In 902 the three sons of Countess Adelindis were killed while trying to abduct their sister, the abbess, to marry her. The countess entered the abbey to atone and is still honoured as a benefactress. A mint and market are mentioned in 1014 or 1022, and in 1417 the abbey became a secular convent that accepted daughters of the Swabian nobility.</p><p>A Jewish community is recorded in the city from 1577 under its protection, with a cemetery built in 1650 and synagogues erected in 1730 and 1837. In 1802 and 1803 both abbey and city lost their independence and passed to the Prince of Thurn and Taxis, and in 1806 Buchau became a township of the Kingdom of Württemberg. The community gained its civil liberties in 1828. Hermann Einstein, father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-einstein">Albert Einstein</a>, was born there in 1847. In 1938 the synagogue was burned and its stone later sold for road building. The Jewish people of Buchau, who had made up a substantial part of the population, were deported from 1941, and only four returned after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The town, with about 4,000 inhabitants, is in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>, and its people speak the Swabian dialect in daily life. It was given the title Bad, meaning spa, in 1963, after the civic spa opened in 1949. Excavations in the moor beginning around the early 20th century uncovered the Wasserburg Buchau, a settlement of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>, about 1100 to 800 BCE. The Federsee Museum opened in 1968 with exhibits from the Stone and Bronze Ages. The prehistoric settlement at Siedlung Forschner is part of the Prehistoric Pile Dwellings around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bad Buchau” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Buchau</b> grew on an island in the Federsee in upper Swabia beside the abbey of noble canonesses founded there in the 8th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-buchau">Free Imperial City of Buchau</a></li><li><blockquote>The abbey church of Saints Cornelius and Cyprian survives in <b>Bad Buchau</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/imperial-abbey-of-buchau">Imperial Abbey of Buchau</a></li></ul></section>
